Of course! The best way to handle this is with a regular expression, as it can easily find the pattern `(age ...)` even when the number inside changes.

Here are a few ways to do it, starting with the most recommended method.

### Method 1: Using a Regular Expression (Recommended)

This is the most robust and concise solution. It finds the pattern `(age` followed by one or more digits, enclosed in parentheses, and replaces it.

```javascript
const originalString = "july 7 1994 (age 7) London, UK (1994-07-07)";
const anotherString = "Jan 1 2010 (age 14) New York, US (2010-01-01)";

// The regular expression to find "(age [any number])" and any space after it.
// Breakdown of the regex: /\(age\s+\d+\)\s*/
// \(       - Matches the literal opening parenthesis
// age      - Matches the literal word "age"
// \s+      - Matches one or more whitespace characters (like space, tab)
// \d+      - Matches one or more digits (0-9)
// \)       - Matches the literal closing parenthesis
// \s*      - Matches zero or more whitespace characters (to remove the space after)
const regex = /\(age\s+\d+\)\s*/;

// Use the replace() method with the regex
const newString1 = originalString.replace(regex, "");
const newString2 = anotherString.replace(regex, "");

console.log(`Original: "${originalString}"`);
console.log(`Cleaned:  "${newString1}"`);
// Expected Output: "july 7 1994 London, UK (1994-07-07)"

console.log("---");

console.log(`Original: "${anotherString}"`);
console.log(`Cleaned:  "${newString2}"`);
// Expected Output: "Jan 1 2010 New York, US (2010-01-01)"
```

### Creating a Reusable Function

It's good practice to wrap this logic in a function so you can easily reuse it.

```javascript
function removeAgeFromString(text) {
  // Return the original text if it's not a string
  if (typeof text !== 'string') {
    return text;
  }

  const regex = /\(age\s+\d+\)\s*/;
  return text.replace(regex, "");
}

// --- Examples ---
const string1 = "july 7 1994 (age 7) London, UK (1994-07-07)";
const string2 = "May 25 1980 (age 44) Toronto, CA (1980-05-25)";
const string3 = "No age here, just a date (2024-01-01)";

console.log(removeAgeFromString(string1));
// Output: july 7 1994 London, UK (1994-07-07)

console.log(removeAgeFromString(string2));
// Output: May 25 1980 Toronto, CA (1980-05-25)

console.log(removeAgeFromString(string3));
// Output: No age here, just a date (2024-01-01)
```

### Method 2: Using String Manipulation (Without Regex)

This method is more verbose and less flexible than a regular expression but can be easier to understand if you're not familiar with regex.

```javascript
function removeAgeWithoutRegex(text) {
  const startMarker = "(age ";
  const endMarker = ")";

  const startIndex = text.indexOf(startMarker);

  // If the "(age " part is not found, return the original string
  if (startIndex === -1) {
    return text;
  }

  const endIndex = text.indexOf(endMarker, startIndex);

  // If the closing ")" is not found after the start, return original string
  if (endIndex === -1) {
    return text;
  }

  // Get the parts of the string before and after the "age" text
  const before = text.substring(0, startIndex);
  const after = text.substring(endIndex + 1);

  // Join them and use trim() to remove extra spaces at the join point
  return (before + after).replace(/\s{2,}/g, ' '); // Clean up double spaces
}

// --- Example ---
const originalString = "july 7 1994 (age 7) London, UK (1994-07-07)";
const newString = removeAgeWithoutRegex(originalString);

console.log(newString);
// Output: july 7 1994 London, UK (1994-07-07)
```

For this specific problem, **Method 1 (Regular Expression) is strongly recommended** because it's precisely what regular expressions were designed for: finding and replacing patterns in text.

